    "result_text": "I added the `ratelimit` decorator and applied it to login, register and both team forms. I didn't run the app or its tests.\n\n`CTFd/utils/decorators/__init__.py` imported and used `ratelimit` in several places but never defined it. I added it from the catalog pattern `decorators.ratelimit@v1`, with the `# pcat:` marker kept above it. It counts requests per client IP and endpoint in the shared cache. Once the limit is reached, it returns a JSON 429 until the interval expires. It only counts requests whose method matches, which is `POST` by default.\n\n| Form | Location | Limit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Login | `auth.py` `login()` | 10 per 5s (already decorated) |\n| Register | `auth.py` `register()` | 10 per 5s (already decorated) |\n| Join team | `teams.py` `join()` | 10 per 5s (already decorated) |\n| New team | `teams.py` `new()` | 10 per 5s (**added**) |\n\nThe team invite form (`/teams/invite`) is not rate-limited. You only named login, register and the team forms, so I left it alone. It's a one-line addition if you want it covered.\n\n`check_code` reports no deviations from the pattern. My first check flagged four high-severity deviations, but that was because I submitted the code without its import lines. Re-running with the real imports came back clean, so no `pcat-allow` approvals are needed."
